<Base title="How it runs" description="How Lookahead estimates a query before DuckDB executes it: scan estimates, four verdict states, DuckDB execution, open table storage, and a local record.">
  <PageIntro label="02 / How it runs" title="See the work." accent="Then make the call." description="Lookahead is your query gauge. Before execution, it reads the query plan and table metadata. It estimates what this machine will need, and tells you when an estimate is unavailable."><a class="button" href="#signals">Understand the verdicts ↓</a><a class="text-link" href={url('/docs/gauge')}>Read the Lookahead guide ↗</a></PageIntro>
  <section class="section-frame pipeline-section"><div class="pipeline" aria-label="Query execution flow"><div><span>01 / YOUR QUESTION</span><strong>SQL</strong><p>From the app, CLI, or API</p></div><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><div><span>02 / BEFORE EXECUTION</span><strong>Lookahead</strong><p>Plan + metadata + machine</p></div><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><div><span>03 / THE ENGINE</span><strong>DuckDB</strong><p>Run locally, stream results</p></div></div><div class="pipeline-foundation"><span>OPEN FOUNDATIONS</span><strong>Parquet + Iceberg tables</strong><span>Local files or attached Parquet in S3</span></div></section>
  <section id="signals" class="web-section section-frame"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Four states. A reason with each.</p><h2>A signal you<br /><span class="accent">can act on.</span></h2></div><p>Estimates describe the expected work.<br />They are guidance, not a runtime guarantee.</p></div><div class="verdict-grid"><article class="verdict-green"><span>● / GREEN</span><h3>Runs here</h3><p>The estimated work fits within the configured time and memory thresholds.</p></article><article class="verdict-yellow"><span>● / YELLOW</span><h3>Runs here, slowly</h3><p>The estimate expects a longer run, high memory use, or spilling to disk.</p></article><article class="verdict-red"><span>● / RED</span><h3>Needs more machine</h3><p>The query is refused by default. You can change the query or explicitly choose to run it anyway.</p></article><article><span>○ / NO ESTIMATE</span><h3>Not estimated</h3><p>A scan is outside the catalog’s statistics. Lakelet says what it cannot estimate; the query can still run.</p></article></div></section>
  <DataStory />
  <section class="split-section section-frame"><div><p class="eyebrow">What goes into the estimate</p><h2>More than<br />a table’s size.</h2><p>The interactive example above isolates transfer time. Lookahead also considers the plan’s operators, memory, CPU, and disk spill.</p></div><div class="web-accordions"><details open><summary>Columns and filters <span>+</span></summary><p>Projected columns reduce the bytes to read. Supported filters can prune files through Iceberg manifests. The estimate reports whether pruning was fully or partly understood.</p></details><details><summary>Memory and spill <span>+</span></summary><p>Joins, aggregations, and sorts need working memory. The gauge estimates peak memory and accounts for writing to disk when the work spills.</p></details><details><summary>Your machine and the limits <span>+</span></summary><p>RAM, free disk, thread count, disk throughput, and remote bandwidth inform the estimate. Timing constants were tuned on one machine; per-machine correction is planned.</p><a href={url('/docs/gauge')}>See calibration and limitations ↗</a></details><details><summary>A record after the run <span>+</span></summary><p>History records the verdict, estimate, and actuals locally. You can inspect the gap between the prediction and what happened.</p></details></div></section>
  <section class="notice-band section-frame"><span class="eyebrow">Beyond your machine / Planned</span><h3>Bursting is the next part of the story.</h3><p>Cloud workers and cost caps are planned. Any worker estimate in today’s core is arithmetic from the plan; it does not launch a worker.</p><a class="text-link" href={url('/pricing')}>See the planned offering ↗</a></section><NextStep />
</Base>
